<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <main class="sasha-practice-main">

    <div class="sasha-practice-content">


      <!-- ============================================
           HEADER
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-header">

        <p class="sasha-practice-eyebrow">
          Sasha Best Practice
        </p>

        <h1>
          Personnel Experience Evidence Review
        </h1>

        <p class="sasha-practice-intro">
          Review the experience of proposed personnel before
          writing begins so the proposal can make deliberate,
          evidence-based arguments about why this team is the
          right team for the assignment.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHY CONDUCT THE REVIEW
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Why conduct a personnel experience review?
        </h2>

        <p>
          A proposal should do more than provide resumes for
          the people assigned to the project.
        </p>

        <p>
          It should demonstrate why their particular
          experience matters to this client and this
          assignment.
        </p>

        <p>
          The personnel experience review examines the
          available evidence before writing begins and asks
          whether the proposed team's experience supports
          the arguments the proposal intends to make.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           START WITH THE RFP
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Start with what the RFP asks for
        </h2>

        <p>
          Begin by reviewing the RFP requirements and
          evaluation criteria relating to personnel.
        </p>

        <p>
          Look for requirements concerning:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            specific roles or positions;
          </li>

          <li>
            required qualifications or certifications;
          </li>

          <li>
            years or types of experience;
          </li>

          <li>
            experience on comparable assignments;
          </li>

          <li>
            experience with the client or jurisdiction;
          </li>

          <li>
            availability and commitment;
          </li>

          <li>
            team organization and responsibilities; and
          </li>

          <li>
            any personnel criteria that will be formally
            scored.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          These requirements establish the minimum evidence
          the proposal must provide.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           BRING IN ZOE'S EVIDENCE
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Bring in the personnel evidence
        </h2>

        <p>
          Zoe is the firm's source for personnel experience.
          She helps develop and maintain the evidence
          contained in resumes, biographies, qualifications,
          project histories, and other personnel records.
        </p>

        <p>
          That evidence should be available before the
          proposal team decides how each person's experience
          will be presented.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sasha can then help review Zoe's evidence against
          the pursuit requirements and strategy.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHAT SASHA DOES
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          What Sasha does with the evidence
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sasha looks at personnel experience through the
          lens of the current pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          She can help the team compare the available
          personnel evidence with:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            the RFP requirements;
          </li>

          <li>
            the evaluation criteria;
          </li>

          <li>
            the client's priorities and concerns;
          </li>

          <li>
            the proposed roles and responsibilities;
          </li>

          <li>
            the proposal's win themes; and
          </li>

          <li>
            the work each person is expected to perform.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          Sasha can then help determine which experience
          should support which proposal arguments — without
          replacing Zoe's role as the firm's source of
          personnel experience.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHAT TO LOOK FOR
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          What to look for during the review
        </h2>

        <p>
          The strongest personnel evidence is not necessarily
          the longest resume or the greatest number of years
          of experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          Look for evidence that demonstrates a meaningful
          connection between the person's past experience and
          the work required for this pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          Consider:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            <strong>Role relevance.</strong>
            Has the person successfully performed the role
            being proposed?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Comparable experience.</strong>
            Have they worked on projects with similar scope,
            complexity, conditions, stakeholders, or risks?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Specific responsibilities.</strong>
            Does the evidence show what the person actually
            did on those assignments?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Results.</strong>
            Are there outcomes or accomplishments that
            demonstrate the value of their contribution?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Client knowledge.</strong>
            Does the person have relevant experience with
            the client, municipality, agency, community,
            or jurisdiction?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Team continuity.</strong>
            Have proposed team members successfully worked
            together before?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Qualifications.</strong>
            Are required licences, certifications,
            education, or other credentials clearly
            supported?
          </li>

          <li>
            <strong>Availability.</strong>
            Is there credible evidence that the person can
            actually perform the proposed role during the
            required period?
          </li>

        </ul>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           LOOK FOR GAPS
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Look for gaps — not just strengths
        </h2>

        <p>
          The review should identify personnel evidence that
          is missing, weak, outdated, or poorly matched to
          the pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          A gap might mean the proposal team needs to:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            ask Zoe for additional personnel experience;
          </li>

          <li>
            confirm a person's role on a past project;
          </li>

          <li>
            obtain stronger project examples;
          </li>

          <li>
            verify a qualification or certification;
          </li>

          <li>
            confirm availability or level of effort;
          </li>

          <li>
            reconsider the role assigned to a team member;
            or
          </li>

          <li>
            consider whether another person provides
            stronger evidence for a critical role.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          Finding those weaknesses during planning gives the
          team time to resolve them before resumes and
          personnel sections are written.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           CONNECT PERSONNEL TO STRATEGY
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Connect personnel experience to the win strategy
        </h2>

        <p>
          The proposed team should reinforce the proposal's
          strategy.
        </p>

        <p>
          For each key person, ask:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            Why is this person on the team?
          </li>

          <li>
            What client concern or project challenge does
            their experience help address?
          </li>

          <li>
            What specific experience proves they can perform
            the proposed role?
          </li>

          <li>
            What makes their experience particularly
            relevant to this assignment?
          </li>

          <li>
            Is there a fact, result, relationship, or
            accomplishment that differentiates them?
          </li>

          <li>
            Where should that evidence appear in the
            proposal?
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          This turns personnel information into proposal
          evidence rather than simply biographical content.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           ZOE AND SASHA
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          Zoe provides the evidence. Sasha helps you use it.
        </h2>

        <p>
          Zoe remains the firm's personnel and resume
          specialist. Her role is to develop, organize,
          and provide reliable evidence about the firm's
          people and their experience.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sasha's role begins when that evidence is brought
          into the pursuit.
        </p>

        <p>
          She helps the proposal team evaluate it in the
          context of the client, the RFP, the evaluation
          criteria, the proposed role, and the win strategy.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The distinction is important:
          </strong>

          <p>
            Zoe answers,
            <em>
              "What experience does this person have?"
            </em>
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha helps answer,
            <em>
              "Why does this person's experience matter
              to this pursuit?"
            </em>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           WHEN TO CONDUCT THE REVIEW
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          When to conduct the personnel review
        </h2>

        <p>
          Conduct the review during proposal planning,
          once the team structure and key roles are becoming
          clear but before resumes and personnel sections
          are written.
        </p>

        <p>
          This leaves time to strengthen the evidence,
          resolve gaps, confirm responsibilities, or adjust
          the proposed team if necessary.
        </p>

        <p>
          Repeat the review when the team changes or when
          new RFP information, addenda, client intelligence,
          or strategy decisions affect personnel
          requirements.
        </p>

      </div>


      <!-- ============================================
           PRACTICAL OUTCOME
      ============================================= -->

      <div class="sasha-practice-section">

        <h2>
          What you should have when the review is complete
        </h2>

        <p>
          At the end of the review, the proposal team should
          understand:
        </p>

        <ul>

          <li>
            why each key person is being proposed;
          </li>

          <li>
            the strongest evidence supporting that person's
            role;
          </li>

          <li>
            which RFP requirements and evaluation criteria
            their experience addresses;
          </li>

          <li>
            how their experience supports the win strategy;
          </li>

          <li>
            which facts and accomplishments should be
            emphasized in the proposal; and
          </li>

          <li>
            where personnel evidence still needs to be
            strengthened.
          </li>

        </ul>

        <p>
          The result should be a team whose experience is
          not merely presented, but deliberately connected
          to the reasons the client should select the firm.
        </p>

      </div>


